  "scenes": [
    {
      "narration": "Earn the same salary in four English-speaking countries, and you take home four very different amounts.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "hook",
          "art": "globe",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ],
          "lines": [
            "1 salary.",
            "4 take-homes."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s1"
    },
    {
      "narration": "On income tax alone, the US often looks the cheapest. But that's only half the story.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "rows",
          "kicker": "Total tax burden",
          "rows": [
            {
              "rank": "1",
              "name": "Australia",
              "value": "highest",
              "note": "but services included",
              "color": "#17120f",
              "bar": 0.9
            },
            {
              "rank": "2",
              "name": "UK / Canada",
              "value": "high",
              "note": "healthcare covered",
              "color": "#17120f",
              "bar": 0.8
            },
            {
              "rank": "3",
              "name": "USA",
              "value": "lowest tax",
              "note": "you pay privately",
              "color": "#c0392b",
              "bar": 0.55
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Headline income tax is only one slice of the total burden.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-tax",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · tax"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s3"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The hidden one is sales tax. The US has no national one, the UK adds twenty percent VAT.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "globe",
          "big": "0% vs 20%",
          "label": "national sales tax: US vs UK",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "x"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s4"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A twenty percent VAT quietly taxes almost everything you buy.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-vat",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · value-added tax"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s5"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Measured as a share of the whole economy, the US takes about twenty-seven percent, the others around thirty-three.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "globe",
          "big": "27% vs 33%",
          "label": "total tax, share of economy",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "But lower tax means Americans pay privately for healthcare and more.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-wedge",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · tax wedge"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s7"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Low tax usually just means you quietly pay privately for what others get through theirs.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "Pay it privately",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s8"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The headline tax rate hides what each system actually covers.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-rate",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · tax rate"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s9"
    },
    {
      "narration": "So all in, which country's deal would you actually pick?",
